---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getMeta, getModel, int, num, pct } from '../lib/data';

const meta = getMeta();
const model = getModel();
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

// Options tested and rejected, each with the measurement that decided it. An
// untested assertion is worth nothing; a tested rejection is worth reporting.
const alternatives = [
  ['Cursor pagination inside a date window', 'Rejected', 'The cursor does not advance — page 2 returns an identical cursor and 0 new rows. Verified on two games.'],
  ['Cursor-then-split hybrid collection', 'Rejected', '26.0 reviews per request against 32.7 for splitting alone. The confirming request per window costs more than it saves.'],
  ['Size-routed cursor / window collection', 'Adopted', '99.9 reviews per request on small games; time windows only where a full history walk cannot reach the early-access period.'],
  ['Steam\'s store release date as the EA boundary', 'Rejected', `Wrong for ${pct(meta.data_quality.release_date_wrong_share, 1)} of the titles it can be checked against by more than 7 days, with a median error of ${num(meta.data_quality.release_date_median_error_days, 0)} days. Replaced with a bisect on the per-review early-access flag.`],
  ['Multilingual toxicity model (XLM-RoBERTa)', 'Rejected', 'Would raise coverage to ~71%, but two differently-calibrated models across games injects a model artefact into the between-game comparison the score is made of.'],
  ['Raw review text vs cleaned text for scoring', 'Adopted cleaned', 'Both scored; the delta is reported. Markup, emoji and foreign script removed, punctuation and casing kept because both scorers read them.'],
  ['Min–max normalisation', 'Rejected', 'Playtime and owner distributions are extremely heavy-tailed. Replaced with percentile/robust scaling, fitted inside the training fold only.'],
  ['Random train/test split', 'Rejected', 'Leaks the future. Replaced with a temporal split on the derived launch date, which is what deployment actually looks like.'],
  ['SteamSpy owner estimates', 'Rejected', 'Unreachable behind Cloudflare, and unreliable since Valve\'s 2018 privacy change. Replaced with official Valve data.'],
  ['Perspective API as the toxicity scorer', 'Deferred', 'A ~1 QPS quota makes a full-corpus pass a multi-day job. Named as future work: a 1–2k stratified agreement subsample.'],
];
---
<Base title="Methodology" section="methodology"
      description="How the Early-Access Health Score is built, and the alternatives tested and rejected.">

  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="prose secondary">
    The short version: collect every early-access review a game has, measure whether the people
    who wrote them kept playing, measure how they wrote, and check whether either predicts what
    happened after launch.
  </p>

  <h2>The metric this study contributes</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>Post-Review Persistence</strong> is a reviewer's total playtime minus their playtime
    at the moment they reviewed — how much longer they kept playing after writing. Steam returns
    both numbers on every review, so it costs nothing to compute and needs no private telemetry.
    Aggregated per game it is a public-data retention proxy, and it does not appear in the
    existing literature.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    It is <strong>right-censored</strong>. Playtime is a single snapshot taken on
    {' '}{meta.collection_date}, so a game that launched recently has had less time to accrue it
    than one that launched in 2018. That is a real limitation, not a rounding issue, and it is
    why the correlation with the post-launch outcome (ρ={model.persistence_rho.toFixed(3)}) is described as association
    rather than prediction.
  </p>

  <h2>How the score is built</h2>
  <ol class="prose steps">
    <li><strong>Collect.</strong> Every review for {int(meta.corpus.n_games_collected)} qualifying
      games — {int(meta.corpus.n_reviews_collected)} in total — rate-limited and resumable.</li>
    <li><strong>Find the real boundary.</strong> Steam's store release date is wrong for most
      early-access titles, so the early-access → launch boundary is found by binary-searching the
      point where the per-review early-access flag flips. Accurate to about a fortnight.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean.</strong> English-only ({pct(meta.corpus.english_share_overall)} of the
      corpus), markup and emoji stripped, authors hashed. Steam's own profanity redaction is
      mapped to a token rather than deleted — the redaction is itself the signal.</li>
    <li><strong>Score the text.</strong> Detoxify for toxicity, VADER for sentiment, capped at a
      stratified 2,000 reviews per game.</li>
    <li><strong>Aggregate to one row per game.</strong> Persistence, velocity, review-score
      trajectory, toxicity level and trend, sentiment. Raw values only — no scaling at this
      stage, so scaling cannot leak across the split.</li>
    <li><strong>Fit and evaluate.</strong> Temporal split on the derived launch date. All
      normalisation and weights fitted inside the training fold. Baselines first, then the score.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Two weighting schemes, both reported</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The score is an equal-weight average of an engagement half and a community half. A
    data-driven weighting from logistic coefficients is computed alongside it and reported next
    to it everywhere. Neither is presented as the single right answer, because choosing one
    silently would hide a real modelling decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Inclusion criteria</h2>
  <p class="prose secondary">
    A documented early-access period, multiplayer, at least
    {' '}{meta.frame.min_ea_reviews} early-access reviews, and — for the modelling frame — a full
    release at least {meta.frame.post_launch_window_days} days before collection with at least
    {' '}{meta.frame.min_post_launch_reviews} post-launch reviews. The outcome window is fixed at
    {' '}{meta.frame.post_launch_window_days} days so a 2018 launch and a 2024 launch are measured
    on the same clock.
  </p>
  <p class="prose secondary">
    Games that <em>never</em> left early access fail those criteria by construction — which is
    exactly the survivorship problem. They are collected and scored anyway, kept out of the
    modelling frame, and analysed separately on the
    {' '}<a href={`${base}/performance/#survivorship`}>results page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alternatives">Alternatives tested and rejected</h2>
  <p class="prose secondary">
    Each row was measured before it was decided. The measurement is the reason.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {alternatives.map(([opt, verdict, why]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{opt}</strong></td>
            <td class="verdict-cell">{verdict}</td>
            <td class="secondary">{why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What this cannot tell you</h2>
  <p class="prose">{meta.proxy_statement}</p>
  <ul class="prose secondary">
    <li>It says nothing about in-game behaviour. Chat and voice are private; reviews are what is public.</li>
    <li>It says nothing about game quality, and a low score is not a claim that a game is bad.</li>
    <li>It is measured on the {pct(meta.corpus.english_share_overall)} of reviews written in English.</li>
    <li>The toxicity model systematically over-scores gaming slang and banter. Worked examples are on the <a href={`${base}/performance/#limitations`}>results page</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="corrections">Requesting a correction</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    If you develop or publish a game listed here and believe its score misrepresents it, that is
    worth knowing and worth fixing. The most useful things to send are the app ID, what looks
    wrong, and anything about the game's early-access history the public data would get wrong —
    a relaunch, an app-ID change, a review-bombing episode, a long dormant period.
  </p>
  <p class="prose secondary">
    This is a student research artefact rather than a service, so corrections are handled by
    re-running the pipeline rather than by editing a number by hand. Contact details are in the
    report and the project repository. Every score here regenerates from public data with one
    command, so a correction to the method fixes every affected game at once.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .steps { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; max-width: 70ch; }
  .verdict-cell { white-space: nowrap; font-weight: 600; }
  td.secondary { max-width: 46ch; }
</style>
